course: dictionary
lesson: 5864
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one to use. Management can mean organising something, such as your time or your money. It can also mean the group of people in charge, the bosses.
explain: Look at the words around it. «Time management» is about planning how you use your hours, so here the word means organising. In «the management decided to open on Sundays», the word the comes before it and the bosses are the ones deciding, so here it means the people in charge.
explain: Some words are jargon, a special language used by a certain group such as doctors, teachers or businesspeople. «"Behaviour management" is jargon for discipline and punishment.» So in a school, it means how adults keep order and deal with bad behaviour. Always check: swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
fact: Management means controlling and organising something.
fact: «Time management» means planning how you use your time well.
fact: «The management» means the group of people in charge of a company, shop or team.
fact: «Behaviour management» is jargon for discipline and punishment.
fact: Management is an uncountable noun, so you do not add an s to it.
exercise: What does management mean in «time management»? | Sleeping through the morning | Being the youngest in the class | *Planning and organising how you use your time
exercise: «The management of the shop decided to open on Sundays.» What does the management mean here? | The shelves and the till | *The people in charge of the shop | Customers who shop on Sundays | A type of sweet
exercise: What is «behaviour management» in a school? | *The way adults keep order and deal with bad behaviour | Planning the school lunch menu | Cleaning the classrooms | Teaching children how to draw
test: Which phrase means the people in charge? | anger management | *the management | money management | time management
test: What does «money management» mean? | *Controlling and organising how you spend and save money | Asking for more pocket money | Counting coins in a jar | Earning money by working
exam: Jayden says management always means the bosses. Why is he wrong? | *Management can also mean controlling and organising something, such as money or time | Management is a kind of sport | Management means only the people who clean the school | Management is a type of food
